Use the manual for the command that will actually run. Two computers
can both have a command named \texttt{stat} and disagree on what its
options mean. Even on one computer, the shell may provide a built-in
command with the same name as an installed utility.

\section{Choose a portability target}

\IndexTerm{POSIX} specifies interfaces and behavior across systems; it
is not a package to install. The POSIX shell language is a useful
common target for short scripts. For an interactive session, use the
shell that suits how you work. To try a POSIX-style example in a shell
with different syntax, start \IndexCommand{sh}, work in that child
shell, and type \texttt{exit} when finished. The command \texttt{sh}
does not identify one universal executable or one universal set of
extensions.

\begin{quicktable}{p{0.88in}YY}
\toprule
\textbf{Shell} & \textbf{Good fit} & \textbf{What not to assume}\\
\midrule
POSIX sh & Small scripts shared across families of Unix systems. &
Arrays, \texttt{[[} tests and \texttt{source} are not portable sh syntax.\\
Bash & Interactive work and scripts that explicitly require Bash. &
An older Bash may lack features available in a current upstream release.\\
Zsh & Interactive completion and configurable filename generation. &
Default expansion behavior is not the same as Bash or POSIX sh.\\
Ksh & Korn-shell scripts and interactive use where available. &
Ksh implementations are not identical; check the installed manual.\\
Fish & Interactive use with its own completion and scripting syntax. &
Use an explicit sh interpreter for sh scripts, not Fish syntax.\\
Csh/Tcsh & Existing C-shell workflows and interactive Tcsh use. &
Assignments and control syntax differ from sh.\\
\bottomrule
\end{quicktable}

When a script is intended for \texttt{sh}, test it with
\cmd|sh script.sh|, not by pasting its lines into a more permissive
shell. When it needs another interpreter, say so in the instructions.
The shell in your terminal and the interpreter for a script need not
be the same.

\section{Prefer shared forms for shared tasks}

\begin{longtable}{p{0.97in}p{1.55in}p{2.25in}}
\toprule
\textbf{Task} & \textbf{Shared starting point} & \textbf{Boundary}\\
\midrule
\endhead
Print text & \texttt{printf '\%s\textbackslash n' text} &
Pass the text as an operand, not as the format string. Do not depend on
\texttt{echo} to handle backslashes or a leading \texttt{-n} consistently.\\
Preview a copy & \texttt{ls -ld source target} &
An absent target can produce a diagnostic. Check that each displayed
object is the one you meant. \texttt{cp -i} prompts before many
overwrites, but a preview also catches the wrong directory.\\
Search literal text & \texttt{grep -F -e text file} &
\texttt{-F} disables regular expressions; \texttt{-e} introduces the
pattern even when it begins with a hyphen. Quote a pattern containing
spaces or shell metacharacters.\\
Sort an ASCII fixture & \texttt{LC\_ALL=C sort file} &
The per-command locale setting is useful for reproducible practice.
Do not mistake this order for alphabetical order in a reader's language.\\
Compare contents & \texttt{cmp file1 file2} &
Status 0 means identical, 1 means different, and a higher status denotes
an error. A successful comparison is not a check of owners or permissions.\\
Edit a stream & \texttt{sed 's/old/new/' in.txt} &
Write to a different path after previewing; in-place options vary and
are not part of the portable form.\\
Measure space & \texttt{df -P .}; \texttt{du -sk .} &
The first reports on a filesystem, the second on reachable entries.
Do not expect their numbers to agree.\\
Check for damage & \texttt{cksum file} &
The default CRC is for accidental corruption checks, not proof of
authenticity. SHA-256 command names and output formats vary.\\
\bottomrule
\end{longtable}

\section{Recognize platform-specific interfaces}

\IndexTerm{GNU utilities} and utilities shipped in the BSDs may share a
portable core without sharing extensions. For example, GNU
\texttt{stat} uses \texttt{-c} to request an output format; the native
\texttt{stat} on macOS and the BSDs uses \texttt{-f} for formatting.
These are different interfaces; substituting the letter alone is not a
translation of the format string. Use a platform's own examples and
manual.

\begin{quicktable}{p{0.82in}YY}
\toprule
\textbf{System}&\textbf{Manuals}&\textbf{What to verify}\\
\midrule
Linux & Installed manuals; GNU manuals for GNU tools. &
A distribution may instead ship BusyBox or another implementation.\\
macOS & Installed manuals; Apple Terminal User Guide for the app. &
An optional GNU tool and Apple's native utility can coexist.\\
FreeBSD & Installed manuals; FreeBSD manual pages and Handbook. &
Base commands and installed packages can have different update cycles.\\
OpenBSD & Installed manuals; OpenBSD manual pages and FAQ. &
Read the documentation for your release before using an extension.\\
NetBSD & Installed manuals; NetBSD manual pages and Guide. &
Base commands and pkgsrc-provided tools are not interchangeable by name alone.\\
\bottomrule
\end{quicktable}

Try \cmd|command -v sort| in a POSIX-style shell to see how that shell
resolves the name. That result is not a version number. Inspect the
local manual and the installed package information before assuming a
feature or scripting around the output. GNU-style \texttt{--version}
and \texttt{--help} are not universal.

